<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section">1. Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used in long-term interactions,
where they must draw on information accumulated across many conversations
and tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>.
The resulting histories can far exceed a model’s context window, yet relevant
past information must remain accessible for future responses.
To address this challenge, recent research has developed a range of agentic
memory systems that store, organize, and retrieve information beyond the
active context <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.
Memory management includes extraction, summarization, mutation, consolidation,
clustering, graph construction, and community discovery, while retrieval may
involve iterative search or trainable retrieval strategies
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>.
These developments raise a basic question: <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">when is the added complexity of
agentic memory systems<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">1</span></span>
              
              
              
            <span id="footnote1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">Here, we use </span><em id="footnote1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_medium ltx_font_italic">agentic memory system</em><span id="footnote1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> as an umbrella term for memory systems encompassing agentic content processing, structural organization, and retrieval control beyond a fixed raw-history retrieval pipeline.</span></span></span></span></span>
<span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">necessary?</span> To investigate, we start with a simple
alternative: retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) over raw history.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">RAG is often considered insufficient for long-term memory
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. Two common objections to raw-history retrieval motivate our investigation.
The first is conceptual: RAG is commonly
associated with static corpora, whereas long-term interactions involve
superseded facts, deletions, and dependent state changes. Can retrieving history
suffice without explicitly maintaining current state? The second is empirical:
prior evaluations report RAG trailing agentic memory systems
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These concerns motivate a closer examination of memory management.
We first ask how often memory tasks involve dynamic changes, how
complex those changes are, and whether they exceed what reader LLMs can
handle directly from retrieved evidence.
Two preliminary experiments examine explicit state maintenance.
We first audit Mem0 on all
LoCoMo conversations and all 500 LongMemEval examples. UPDATE and DELETE
together account for only 4.39% of the 33,902 write decisions on LoCoMo
and 2.46% of the 428,858 write decisions on LongMemEval
(Figure <a href="#S0.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>); many proposed updates are duplicates
or malformed. Effective mutations, defined as persisted text-changing updates
or deletions, account for only 1.90% and 1.34% of all decisions.
Replacing UPDATE with append-only ADD and DELETE with NONE raises
LoCoMo accuracy from 67.36% to 69.42%, without reducing any category score
(Table <a href="#S1.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Explicit mutation provides no accuracy gain in this
configuration.
We then test whether raw evidence is sufficient for dynamic conversation on MEME,
which includes exact recall,
aggregation, tracking, deletion, cascade, and invalidation tasks. Readers receive
the evidence available at the queried phase in chronological order, eliminating
retrieval errors but still requiring them to resolve state changes. DeepSeek-V4-Flash reaches
82.32% on cascades and 91.54% on invalidation (Table <a href="#S1.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
Performance varies substantially across readers, but these results show
that difficult transitions can be resolved from sufficient raw evidence.
Together, these diagnostics suggest that capable readers can often resolve
changing states directly from raw evidence.
To examine the empirical concern, we review 14 prior works and identify
coarse retrieval units, restricted
retrieval depth, or absent reranking in several evaluated baselines
(Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Review of Prior RAG Evaluations ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>). These limitations leave open whether the
reported gaps reflect a need for agentic memory systems or weaknesses in the
chosen retrieval baselines.</p>
</div>
