<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> as a diagnostic baseline: naive RAG over raw history,
using only dense retrieval and cross-encoder reranking to select evidence
for a reader <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>.
Across four benchmarks, this simple baseline
ranks first among the compared memory systems in six of seven
benchmark–model settings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions are summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We challenge the presumed necessity of explicit memory mutation:
capable readers can resolve dynamic states from sufficient raw history,
and disabling mutation improves accuracy in the tested configuration.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We establish <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> as a strong reference baseline, ranking first
in six of seven settings across four benchmarks, showing that naive RAG
matches or exceeds the compared agentic memory systems in most settings.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We identify evidence access and relational completeness as key
bottlenecks: retrieving answer values does not ensure sufficient supporting
paths, and complete-store access does not close the gap to raw evidence.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section">2. Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">2.1. Agentic memory systems</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">External-memory architectures organize persistent information through
write, management, and read operations
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>.
Early approaches summarize or reflect on experience streams
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>,
or provide tiered stores and read–write interfaces
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>.
Recent approaches extract and mutate facts or maintain evolving graphs
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>,
consolidate hierarchical or recurrent stores
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>,
or construct selective event-level and abstract representations
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>.
Hybrid and lightweight approaches retain source episodes alongside
derived memories or selectively maintain events and preferences
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>.
Concurrent work studies ReFind, which preserves raw chat logs and gives an
agent iterative lexical search with session, temporal, context-expansion, and
deduplication controls <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. We evaluate a
fixed dense-retrieval and reranking baseline, isolating evidence access
from query-time search control.
We compare these systems with naive RAG for long-term conversation
understanding and diagnose their evidence-access limitations.</p>
</div>
