<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section">3. The RAW-RAG</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our motivation is to test whether agentic memory systems improve
long-term conversation understanding beyond retrieval over raw history.
<span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> (Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.1. Agentic memory systems ‣ 2. Related Work ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(c)) provides a simple reference,
leaving evidence interpretation and state reconstruction to the reader.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> indexes source history without
LLM-based extraction, summarization, graph construction, or mutation.
Each conversation is
serialized with message timestamps and image captions, split into 256-token
windows with 32-token overlap, and embedded in a flat index. These embeddings
support semantic matching while the original window text remains available
as reader evidence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">At query time, the question is embedded and cosine similarity retrieves the
128 closest windows. A cross-encoder jointly scores each question–window
pair for relevance and selects the top 15.
The selected evidence is then supplied with the question to the
reader, which resolves temporal order and relevant state changes when
generating the answer. Top-15 limits the number of selected items.
Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Raw-RAG: Method Details ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> specifies the full pipeline.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section">4. RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents our experiments evaluating <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> against agentic
memory systems, including benchmark comparisons, ablations, and efficiency.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">4.1. Benchmarks and baselines</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate memory-augmented conversation on four benchmarks, namely LoCoMo (10 conversations; 1,540 non-adversarial questions on
single-hop, multi-hop, temporal, and open-domain reasoning)
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, LongMemEval (500 examples on recall, temporal
reasoning, preferences, and updates) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>, MEME
(100 episodes; 1,188 phase-specific questions on dynamic memory updates and complex memory dependency understanding)
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, and MemoryAgentBench (2,071 questions from 14 sources on accurate
retrieval, test-time learning, long-range understanding, and conflict resolution)
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Baselines include EverMemOS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>,
MemOS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, EMem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>,
SimpleMem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, MemU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>,
MemMachine <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>, Mem0 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>,
Memora <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>, Nemori <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>,
MemoryOS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, A-MEM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>,
and RecMem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>,
alongside Full-context.
On LoCoMo and LongMemEval, we evaluate <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> under settings fully aligned
with the corresponding comparison settings, as detailed in
Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.
On MEME and MemoryAgentBench, all systems follow the same evaluation procedure.
Comparisons under aligned evaluation settings
and their scope are presented in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.
Appendices <a href="#A4.SS1" title="D.1. Benchmark descriptions ‣ Appendix D Experimental Implementation and Reproducibility ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a> and <a href="#A4.SS3" title="D.3. Baseline configurations ‣ Appendix D Experimental Implementation and Reproducibility ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.3</span></a> detail
benchmark tasks and baseline settings.
Appendix <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4. Ablation studies ‣ Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a> reports evidence lengths and
5,000-token controls, where <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> remains competitive.</p>
</div>
</section>
