<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">RQ1: Why is <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> designed this way?</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first test the roles of reranking, retrieval depth, and chunk size.
Table <a href="#S4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 4.3. Ablation studies and efficiency ‣ 4. RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and Figure <a href="#S4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 4.3. Ablation studies and efficiency ‣ 4. RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> isolate these
choices on MemoryAgentBench using
20,000 paired, source-stratified bootstrap resamples and Holm correction.
Removing reranking lowers Total by 3.92 points (<math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{Holm}}=0.0012" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>Holm</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0012</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{Holm}}=0.0012</annotation></semantics></math>),
while returning only one item lowers it by 11.73 points. Final-<math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>
values of 5, 10, 30, and 60 stay within 1.3 points of the baseline.
Top-120 loses 14.57 points, driven by
long-range understanding and conflict resolution. Chunk sizes from 128 to 1,024
do not significantly change Total. These results support reranking and
moderate retrieval depth, without requiring the exact default chunk size or <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">RQ2: Can additional memory management improve <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span>?</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate three memory-management operations: atomic-fact extraction,
summary augmentation, and graph-based retrieval expansion.
These variants reuse the chunking, embedding model, and reranker, while
changing the indexed representation or retrieval candidate set.
None improves <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> (48.32%) on the
same 14-source MemoryAgentBench Total (Table <a href="#S4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 4.3. Ablation studies and efficiency ‣ 4. RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, orange rows).
The exact construction and retrieval procedures are given in the
representation and retrieval ablation appendix (Appendix <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3. Memory-management ablations ‣ Appendix C Diagnostic Procedures and Statistical Methods ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>),
with the extraction prompts in the prompt appendix
(Appendix <a href="#A7.SS2" title="G.2. Memory-management variants ‣ Appendix G Prompt Templates ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.2</span></a>).
Atomic-fact extraction reduces test-time learning from 47.68% to 15.37%
and Total by 19.62 points, consistent with loss or disruption of usable
demonstrations (Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Extended Discussion and Limitations ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>).
Summary augmentation largely preserves Accurate Retrieval performance but loses 5.81 points
overall, chiefly on conflict resolution. Graph expansion ties <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> on
long-range understanding yet remains 5.78 points lower overall.
Thus, these three memory-management additions reduce overall accuracy
in this setting.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">RQ3: How efficient is <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span>?</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We finally examine the computational cost of these additions and compare
system-level latency and scaling. On the 11 commonly measured non-conflict
MemoryAgentBench sources, the three additions require 21.6–109.3M
LLM tokens and 2.9–8.8 hours for Add<math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math>Search, versus
12 minutes for <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> (Table <a href="#A1.T32" title="Table 32 ‣ A.6. Efficiency and scalability ‣ Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a>).
We then compare systems on the same 10-episode, 110-question MEME
slice, using one external conversation/search worker and each system’s native
internal parallelism. <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> finishes Add-to-ready plus Search in 305.91
seconds (Table <a href="#S4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 4.3. Ablation studies and efficiency ‣ 4. RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), 5.1<math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster than the next
system and 30.4<math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster than EverMemOS. For scaling, we stream the
same 1,710-message BEAM-1M
conversation approaching one million tokens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>
into each system with one external worker
(Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, right panel). At the endpoint, cumulative Add time
ranges from 0.33 hours for <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> to 26.95 hours for Memora; the next-fastest
completed run, MemOS, takes 3.25 hours. Both studies exclude answer generation
and evaluation; BEAM-1M includes six completed sequential runs.
Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Experimental Implementation and Reproducibility ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> details execution conditions.
On these workloads, <span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> combines competitive accuracy
with lower ingestion and combined ingestion–retrieval costs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
