<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Does <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> perform well because it supplies better evidence,
or because readers use that evidence more effectively? This distinction
matters: improving the reader cannot reliably compensate for missing
evidence, while broader retrieval may not resolve reasoning errors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We measure <em id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">coverage</em>, the fraction of questions with sufficient
retrieved evidence, and <em id="S5.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">utilization</em>, answer accuracy conditional
on sufficient evidence. Coverage is assessed by an LLM judge on
MemoryAgentBench and task-specific deterministic checks on MEME
(Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1. Coverage, utilization, and path retention ‣ Appendix C Diagnostic Procedures and Statistical Methods ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>). Conditioning on coverage
separates evidence availability from subsequent answer generation,
although the system-specific question sets preclude
a controlled comparison of readers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Tables <a href="#S5.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ 5.1. Raw-RAG offsets lower utilization with high coverage ‣ 5. Why Is Raw-RAG Enough in Most Cases? ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> and <a href="#S5.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ 5.1. Raw-RAG offsets lower utilization with high coverage ‣ 5. Why Is Raw-RAG Enough in Most Cases? ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> show that <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span>
achieves the highest coverage on both benchmarks: 45.29% and 76.35%.
On MemoryAgentBench, coverage spans 19.07–45.29%, whereas utilization
spans only 80.00–89.71%. MEME shows the same pattern: coverage varies
by 40.66 points versus 21.04 for utilization. Thus, systems differ
more in supplying sufficient evidence than in answering correctly
once it is supplied. <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> leads in coverage, helping offset its
less-than-best conditional utilization and explaining its strong performance.</p>
</div>
