<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">5.2. Raw history preserves the paths needed for reasoning</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Raw history can preserve not only answer values but also the information
needed to derive them. Useful retrieval therefore includes the order and
dependencies that make one value current.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">On MEME tracking and cascade after-state questions, an LLM jointly
assesses <em id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">result coverage</em> (required answer values) and
<em id="S5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">path coverage</em> (those values plus chronological order or linked
dependency rules and upstream triggers;
Appendix <a href="#A7.SS10" title="G.10. Result and path assessment ‣ Appendix G Prompt Templates ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.10</span></a>).
Table <a href="#S5.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ 5.2. Raw history preserves the paths needed for reasoning ‣ 5. Why Is Raw-RAG Enough in Most Cases? ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> reports <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\mathrm{path}\mid\mathrm{result})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>path</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>result</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\mathrm{path}\mid\mathrm{result})</annotation></semantics></math>:
the fraction of result-covered questions whose retrieved contexts
also contain a complete supporting path. This conditional rate
measures how often successful value retrieval also preserves
the relations and paths needed to use those values.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> achieves the highest conditional path coverage (62.34%),
followed by MemMachine (56.70%); MemU reaches only 9.62%.
Systems that retrieve answer values can still differ substantially
in preserving their supporting relations.</p>
</div>
