<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">5.3. Preserving raw evidence supports downstream recovery</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The preceding analyses identify missing evidence and incomplete dependency
paths in retrieved contexts, but do not establish where these gaps arise.
Relevant information may remain in memory but escape retrieval, or it may
have been discarded or distorted by ingestion-time processing, such as
replacing source passages with extracted facts or compressed summaries.
We therefore ask: <em id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">can improved access to
the stored memories recover performance, or do these ingestion-time
operations leave information gaps that later retrieval cannot repair?</em></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We compare three conditions on MEME.
<em id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Native</em> uses each system’s ordinary retrieval and readout.
The <em id="S5.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">complete-store oracle</em> uses gold-informed selection over all
exported phase-visible memories.
The <em id="S5.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">raw-evidence control</em> selects original annotated support sessions
using the same gold information. Both controls share a value-free task
policy and a 5,000-token packing budget.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ 5.2. Raw history preserves the paths needed for reasoning ‣ 5. Why Is Raw-RAG Enough in Most Cases? ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> shows that the complete-store oracle
improves all seven systems, but the recovered performance varies across
stored representations. <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> and MemMachine, which retain raw
conversational content in their evaluated stores, reach 57.54% and
57.46%, respectively, compared with 61.60% for raw evidence.
These results are consistent with a role for information preservation during
ingestion in downstream recovery.
The two source-preserving systems recover performance closest to the
raw-evidence control, whereas access to the complete transformed stores
leaves substantially larger deficits. Removing the packing ceiling leaves
all seven stores 7.58–29.38 points below raw evidence, with all paired
95% intervals for these gaps excluding zero.
However, the residual gap is not a pure
measure of information loss during ingestion: even <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> falls
short, and evidence assembly and positional effects such as <em id="S5.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">lost in
the middle</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> may also contribute.
Appendix <a href="#A3.SS5" title="C.5. Complete-store and oracle evaluations ‣ Appendix C Diagnostic Procedures and Statistical Methods ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.5</span></a> specifies the controls;
Appendix <a href="#A1.SS5.SSS2" title="A.5.2. Complete-store evaluation ‣ A.5. Diagnostic analyses ‣ Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5.2</span></a> reports the full results.</p>
</div>
