<section id="A1.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic ltx_title_subsubsection">A.1.3. Short-answer evaluation</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This comparison aligns <span id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> with the short-output evaluation
setting used for the Mem0 and Nemori comparisons.
We report LLM-judge accuracy (<math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>), token F1, and BLEU-1 (<math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>) for the
resulting answers.
We keep the results published by Mem0 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> separate from
Nemori’s version-1 re-evaluations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>: even the same named baseline
has different reported scores. Table <a href="#A1.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ Published overall scores. ‣ A.1.3. Short-answer evaluation ‣ A.1. LoCoMo results ‣ Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> gives
overall results, and Tables <a href="#A1.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ Published overall scores. ‣ A.1.3. Short-answer evaluation ‣ A.1. LoCoMo results ‣ Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>
and <a href="#A1.T14" title="Table 14 ‣ Published overall scores. ‣ A.1.3. Short-answer evaluation ‣ A.1. LoCoMo results ‣ Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> give the category-level metrics.
Our evaluation averages three binary judge decisions per answer.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For <span id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span>, <math id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>, F1, and BLEU-1 evaluate the same generated
answers. The lexical metrics follow
Nemori’s definitions. F1 uses sets of
lowercased tokens after replacing periods, commas, exclamation marks, and
question marks with spaces. BLEU-1 uses lowercased word tokenization,
unigram weights, and sentence-level scoring with method-1 smoothing; scores
are averaged over questions.
The <span id="A1.SS1.SSS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> “minimal” rows use GPT-4o-mini and GPT-4.1-mini with the
same shortest-answer instruction and retrieved evidence, obtained using
text-embedding-3-small and Cohere Rerank-v3.5.
The instruction asks for only the entity, date, number, or list needed to
answer the question, without explanation or background.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS3.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Published overall scores.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS3.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS3.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Mem0’s published GPT-4o-mini category scores, in Single / Multi /
Temporal / Open order, are 67.13 / 51.15 / 55.51 / 72.93.
Weighting these by 841 / 282 / 321 / 96 questions gives 62.14,
rather than the published overall score of 66.88. Question-level
judgments are unavailable, so the tables preserve both the published
category scores and the published aggregate without recomputing either.</p>
</div>
