<section id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Comparative performance.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> achieves 49.62% accuracy (2,481/5,000), ranking third among
seven systems behind MemOS and EverMemOS, without leading any individual
scenario. The comparison measures overall system performance with each
system’s native profile configuration; statistical significance remains
untested.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">A.4. Ablation studies</h3>

<div id="A1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section reports retrieval-design and memory-management ablations,
followed by controls on reader-visible evidence. Table <a href="#S4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 4.3. Ablation studies and efficiency ‣ 4. RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>
compares chunking and reranking choices with three added memory-management
operations.
We first give the full final-<math id="A1.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> sweep, then measure actual reader context
and report two distinct 5k controls: a six-system MemoryAgentBench
intervention and <span id="A1.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span>-only interventions on the other three benchmarks.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic ltx_title_subsubsection">A.4.1. Retrieval and memory ablations</h4>

<div id="A1.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The retrieval-depth sweep uses the same MemoryAgentBench setting and
official capability-level averaging as Table <a href="#S4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 4.3. Ablation studies and efficiency ‣ 4. RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.
Its paired intervals compare each final-<math id="A1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> condition with the default
<math id="A1.SS4.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=15</annotation></semantics></math>; statistical procedures are specified in
Appendix <a href="#A3.SS6" title="C.6. Statistical inference ‣ Appendix C Diagnostic Procedures and Statistical Methods ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.6</span></a>.
Totals and differences are computed from unrounded scores and rounded
independently; a displayed difference can therefore differ by 0.01 points
from subtraction of the displayed totals.</p>
</div>
