<section id="A1.SS4.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic ltx_title_subsubsection">A.4.2. Evidence-length analysis</h4>

<div id="A1.SS4.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We measure the evidence actually supplied to the main <span id="A1.SS4.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> readers
before comparing capped conditions. The selected top-<math id="A1.SS4.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> windows can vary
in token count after expansion and formatting.
Native overflow and truncation policies are specified in
Appendix <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3. Context-length policies ‣ Appendix B Raw-RAG: Method Details ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS4.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Measurement scope.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS4.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A1.T20" title="Table 20 ‣ Measurement scope. ‣ A.4.2. Evidence-length analysis ‣ A.4. Ablation studies ‣ Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a> measures the evidence text saved with
the answers underlying the main <span id="A1.SS4.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> results, after display-time
expansion and formatting. Counts include memory headers, timestamps,
separators, and repeated evidence, but exclude the question, fixed
prompt, chat-message framing, and generated answer. For comparability
we count all rows with tiktoken’s o200k_base
encoding across readers. Index construction uses the GPT-4 encoding for its
256-token windows. Length statistics weight questions equally.
Percentiles use linear interpolation.
LoCoMo excludes adversarial questions. Evidence-length distributions
are identical across the two readers on LoCoMo, MEME, and MemoryAgentBench;
only GPT-4.1-mini is used on LongMemEval.</p>
</div>
