<div id="A1.SS4.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A1.T21" title="Table 21 ‣ A.4.3. Cross-system budget control ‣ A.4. Ablation studies ‣ Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a> summarizes the distribution of uncapped
reader-evidence lengths and the effect of the 5,000-token ceiling.
<span id="A1.SS4.SSS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> stays below the ceiling for every question, so its mean evidence
length remains 3,915.8 tokens. EMem and Mem0 have lower median lengths
but long upper tails: their 95th percentiles exceed 5,000 tokens, and
clipping affects 511 and 500 questions, respectively. Their mean lengths
fall to 1,417.7 and 1,961.3 tokens under the ceiling.
EverMemOS and MemMachine supply substantially longer uncapped evidence,
with means of 15,394.8 and 26,296.9 tokens. Clipping affects nearly all
their questions (2,011 and 2,030 of 2,071), reducing their means to
4,160.6 and 4,479.2 tokens. Thus, the same ceiling leaves <span id="A1.SS4.SSS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span>
unchanged, shortens the upper tails of EMem and Mem0, and substantially
reduces evidence length for most EverMemOS and MemMachine questions.</p>
</div>
