<section id="A1.SS4.SSS3.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Interpretation.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS4.SSS3.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.SSS3.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A1.T22" title="Table 22 ‣ A.4.3. Cross-system budget control ‣ A.4. Ablation studies ‣ Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a> reports the completed control.
<span id="A1.SS4.SSS3.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> requires no clipping on any MemoryAgentBench source:
its largest evidence input is 4,262 tokens and its Overall remains
48.32%, versus 44.74% for the strongest baseline in this control.
Overall scores for all five baselines decrease under the historical
5k intervention, although individual sources can improve.
<span id="A1.SS4.SSS3.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> thus remains ahead under the 5k ceiling. Clipping can remove
useful items or disrupt their format, and all systems use their original
tuning. This control covers the six listed systems with GPT-4o-mini.
The controls below extend the evidence ceiling to <span id="A1.SS4.SSS3.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> on the other
three benchmarks.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS4.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic ltx_title_subsubsection">A.4.4. Raw-RAG budget sensitivity</h4>

<div id="A1.SS4.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These controls use the main GPT-4o-mini LoCoMo and MEME conditions
and the GPT-4.1-mini LongMemEval condition. Selected top-15 chunks and
their ordering are frozen. For over-budget questions, turn expansion is
disabled before any clipping is considered; the complete policy is
specified in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS4" title="C.4. Context-budget interventions ‣ Appendix C Diagnostic Procedures and Statistical Methods ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.4</span></a>.
No other baseline is rerun under this policy.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the completed experiments, disabling expansion alone suffices
for every over-budget question: all 15 selected chunks remain, with
no additional chunk dropped or clipped. Contexts change for 41 of
1,540 LoCoMo questions and all 500 LongMemEval and 1,188 MEME
questions. All 3,228 answers and their evaluations are rerun,
including LoCoMo’s 1,499 unchanged-context questions. The reruns use
the same reader aliases and benchmark prompts as their native
conditions. Each answer is evaluated three times, using
GPT-4o-mini-2024-07-18 for LoCoMo, the GPT-4o-mini alias for
LongMemEval, and GPT-4o-2024-11-20 for MEME. LoCoMo and LongMemEval
average binary judgments across questions and repetitions; MEME
uses its six-task macro-average with the original before/after filtering.
Evidence-token counts include formatting but exclude the outer prompt
and question.</p>
</div>
