<section id="A1.SS4.SSS4.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Findings and limitations.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS4.SSS4.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.SSS4.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A1.T23" title="Table 23 ‣ A.4.4. Raw-RAG budget sensitivity ‣ A.4. Ablation studies ‣ Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a> shows higher rerun scores on
LoCoMo and LongMemEval and a 1.09-point decrease on MEME.
Relative to Table <a href="#A1.T20" title="Table 20 ‣ Measurement scope. ‣ A.4.2. Evidence-length analysis ‣ A.4. Ablation studies ‣ Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>, mean evidence length
falls by 63.5% on LongMemEval and 65.2% on MEME.
Category-level changes are reported in Table <a href="#A1.T24" title="Table 24 ‣ Baseline comparison. ‣ A.4.4. Raw-RAG budget sensitivity ‣ A.4. Ablation studies ‣ Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>.
Direct verification of all rerun reader inputs confirms agreement
with the selected display policy, the 5,000-token ceiling, and no
recorded answer-generation error responses.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.SSS4.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.SSS4.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Disabling turn expansion shortens the evidence without dropping or clipping
any selected chunk. All answers and judgments are regenerated, so the score
changes also include reader and judge variability. On LoCoMo, the 41
modified contexts contribute <math id="A1.SS4.SSS4.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.065" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.065</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.065</annotation></semantics></math> points, while the 1,499 unchanged
contexts contribute <math id="A1.SS4.SSS4.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.281" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.281</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.281</annotation></semantics></math> points. Most of its observed gain therefore
comes from questions whose evidence did not change. These RAG-only reruns
describe performance under the smaller budget; significance and equivalence
have not been tested. Comparisons with other systems use their original
scores and evidence budgets.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS4.SSS4.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baseline comparison.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS4.SSS4.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.SSS4.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A1.T24" title="Table 24 ‣ Baseline comparison. ‣ A.4.4. Raw-RAG budget sensitivity ‣ A.4. Ablation studies ‣ Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a> gives the native and capped
<span id="A1.SS4.SSS4.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> category scores; baseline scores remain in
Tables <a href="#S2.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 2.2. Retrieval-augmented generation ‣ 2. Related Work ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.2. Main results ‣ 4. RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
The 5k Overall scores exceed the strongest non-RAG memory-system
baseline by 1.30 points on LoCoMo (MemMachine, 88.12), 1.80 points
on LongMemEval (Memora, 87.40), and 1.15 points on MEME
(MemMachine, 37.06), using the original baseline scores.
The category-level results also remain mixed: for example,
<span id="A1.SS4.SSS4.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> (5k) trails EMem on LongMemEval multi-session questions
and MEME aggregation, deletion, and cascades, despite its higher
Overall scores.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T24" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 24: </span><span id="A1.T24.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> accuracy (%) by task with native evidence and a
5,000-token cap (fixed). For LoCoMo, Single, Multi, Temp., and Open denote
single-hop, multi-hop, temporal, and open-domain questions.
For LongMemEval, S-U., S-A., and S-P. denote single-session user,
assistant, and preference questions; M-S., multi-session; KU., knowledge
update; TR., temporal reasoning. For MEME, ER denotes exact recall;
