<section id="A1.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">A.5. Diagnostic analyses</h3>

<div id="A1.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The diagnostics address different questions: whether a particular mutation
policy helps long-term conversation understanding, whether readers can reconstruct state from supplied source
evidence, whether retrieved contexts contain sufficient evidence and paths,
and what changes under privileged access to stored representations.
Each diagnostic has its own evidence access and reader conditions,
defined with the statistical methods in
Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Diagnostic Procedures and Statistical Methods ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The main text reports the mutation audit
(Figure <a href="#S0.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and Table <a href="#S1.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), source oracle
(Table <a href="#S1.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), and result/path assessment (Table <a href="#S5.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ 5.2. Raw history preserves the paths needed for reasoning ‣ 5. Why Is Raw-RAG Enough in Most Cases? ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>).
We next report uncertainty and complete-store results.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS5.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic ltx_title_subsubsection">A.5.1. Coverage and utilization</h4>

<div id="A1.SS5.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS5.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Tables <a href="#S5.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ 5.1. Raw-RAG offsets lower utilization with high coverage ‣ 5. Why Is Raw-RAG Enough in Most Cases? ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> and <a href="#S5.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ 5.1. Raw-RAG offsets lower utilization with high coverage ‣ 5. Why Is Raw-RAG Enough in Most Cases? ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> give the main estimates.
The following tables supply omnibus tests, confidence intervals, and all
pairwise comparisons for the same evidence assessments. Coverage and utilization
are defined in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1. Coverage, utilization, and path retention ‣ Appendix C Diagnostic Procedures and Statistical Methods ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>; resampling units,
test choices, and multiplicity correction are specified in
Appendix <a href="#A3.SS6" title="C.6. Statistical inference ‣ Appendix C Diagnostic Procedures and Statistical Methods ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.6</span></a>.
The omnibus comparison uses Cochran’s <math id="A1.SS5.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> for MemoryAgentBench coverage
and the centered max-range bootstrap for the other three assessments.
Pairwise tests use exact McNemar tests for MemoryAgentBench coverage and
two-sided bootstrap sign tests for utilization; both MEME metrics use
episode-clustered two-sided bootstrap sign tests.</p>
</div>
