<section id="A1.SS5.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Packing-cap sensitivity.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS5.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS5.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To test whether the nominal 5,000-token packing ceiling drives the
complete-store results, we repeat the diagnostic with that ceiling removed
while holding the exported candidate set, gold-informed selector, ordering,
task policy, reader, and scoring fixed. The raw control is subjected to the
same intervention. Table <a href="#A1.T30" title="Table 30 ‣ Packing-cap sensitivity. ‣ A.5.2. Complete-store evaluation ‣ A.5. Diagnostic analyses ‣ Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30</span></a> reports paired
results over the same 100 episodes and 1,188 question IDs. “Affected” counts
questions whose assembled reader evidence changes; dropped tokens are counted
before the outer prompt and question are added.</p>
</div>
