<div id="A1.SS5.SSS2.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS5.SSS2.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">All seven stores and the raw control have complete results for all 1,188
questions. Removing the ceiling lowers MemMachine’s Overall from 57.46%
to 54.02%, a change of <math id="A1.SS5.SSS2.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-3.44" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3.44</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-3.44</annotation></semantics></math> points [<math id="A1.SS5.SSS2.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-6.49" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6.49</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-6.49</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS5.SSS2.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.37" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.37</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.37</annotation></semantics></math>]; the other
six stores’ <math id="A1.SS5.SSS2.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\infty}-C_{5k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\infty}-C_{5k}</annotation></semantics></math> intervals include zero. MemMachine’s
mean assembled evidence grows from 4,982.8 to 29,459.7 tokens, with no
context-window failures. Its uncapped ER, Agg, Tr, Del, Cas, and Abs
scores are 95.00, 50.00, 55.00, 61.00, 46.95, and 16.15%, respectively.
On the subset whose reader evidence changes, MemU improves by 2.70 points
[0.30, 5.23], whereas MemMachine declines by 3.46 points
[<math id="A1.SS5.SSS2.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-6.49" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6.49</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-6.49</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS5.SSS2.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.40</annotation></semantics></math>]; the other subset intervals include zero.
These subset results describe questions with changed inputs. Every
raw-minus-uncapped-store interval remains positive. The residual gap in
Table <a href="#A1.T29" title="Table 29 ‣ Evaluation scope. ‣ A.5.2. Complete-store evaluation ‣ A.5. Diagnostic analyses ‣ Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a> therefore cannot be attributed solely to
the nominal packing ceiling.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS5.SSS2.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Content-filter sensitivity.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS5.SSS2.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS5.SSS2.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The full results count nine content-filtered reader responses for
<span id="A1.SS5.SSS2.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> and seven for MemMachine as empty, incorrect answers.
As a sensitivity check, we exclude the union of filtered questions and
their paired before/after questions used in gating from every condition: 12
questions in total, leaving 687 after-phase questions. On this common
subset, MemMachine changes from 57.46% to 54.85%, or <math id="A1.SS5.SSS2.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-2.61" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2.61</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2.61</annotation></semantics></math> points
[<math id="A1.SS5.SSS2.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-5.56" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5.56</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-5.56</annotation></semantics></math>, +0.36], and <span id="A1.SS5.SSS2.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> changes from 57.54% to 54.68%,
or <math id="A1.SS5.SSS2.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-2.86" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2.86</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2.86</annotation></semantics></math> points [<math id="A1.SS5.SSS2.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-6.92" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6.92</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-6.92</annotation></semantics></math>, +1.28]; the raw control is 61.53%.
All seven cap-removal intervals now include zero. Thus MemMachine’s
negative full-set interval is sensitive to provider filtering.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS5.SSS2.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Condition alignment.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS5.SSS2.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS5.SSS2.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A1.T29" title="Table 29 ‣ Evaluation scope. ‣ A.5.2. Complete-store evaluation ‣ A.5. Diagnostic analyses ‣ Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a> uses the same Native conditions as
Figure <a href="#S5.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ 5.2. Raw history preserves the paths needed for reasoning ‣ 5. Why Is Raw-RAG Enough in Most Cases? ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>. RAG Native averages three judge
decisions per answer (39.30%); the first decision alone gives 39.80%.
EMem, EverMemOS, and MemMachine Native answers
are scored with GPT-4o. Each <math id="A1.SS5.SSS2.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C-N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C-N</annotation></semantics></math> comparison pairs question-level
scores from the stated Native condition with its complete-store
condition. Mem0 uses the local memory system (27.76%); its cloud
counterpart scores 29.12%. MemOS uses fine-grained extraction.
MemU uses local item-only memory (Appendix <a href="#A4.SS3" title="D.3. Baseline configurations ‣ Appendix D Experimental Implementation and Reproducibility ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.3</span></a>).</p>
