<section id="A1.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">A.6. Efficiency and scalability</h3>

<div id="A1.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We distinguish system-level latency, scaling with history length, and the
cost of additional memory-management operations. Table <a href="#S4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 4.3. Ablation studies and efficiency ‣ 4. RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> reports
Add-to-ready and Search for the same 10-conversation, 110-question MEME
slice. Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> reports cumulative sequential
ingestion over one 1,710-message BEAM-1M conversation. Both use one
outer worker and allow each system’s native internal parallelism;
neither includes answer generation or evaluation. Timing boundaries,
readiness rules, and execution environments are detailed in
Appendices <a href="#A4.SS6" title="D.6. Latency measurement ‣ Appendix D Experimental Implementation and Reproducibility ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.6</span></a> and <a href="#A4.SS7" title="D.7. Computational environment ‣ Appendix D Experimental Implementation and Reproducibility ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.7</span></a>.</p>
