<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Raw-RAG: Method Details</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix details the <span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> pipeline, from source-preserving
indexing and retrieval to evidence assembly and reader input construction.
We describe query construction, benchmark-specific adaptations, and
the distinction between retrieval selection and reader-visible evidence.
We also specify native context-length and overflow policies, separating
them from the diagnostic and context-budget interventions.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">B.1. Indexing and evidence assembly</h3>

<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Source-preserving representation.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=(m_{1},\ldots,m_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=(m_{1},\ldots,m_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the available history. Each message is
represented by its date, speaker, and content; missing dates are marked
as unknown. Supplied image captions enter as text.
“Raw” denotes preservation of this source text. Token windows have length 256 and stride
<math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256-32=224" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>224</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256-32=224</annotation></semantics></math>, measured with the GPT-4 tokenizer. Each window remains
linked to its original messages so that complete turns can be recovered
when assembling evidence for the reader.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Retrieval and evidence assembly.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For question <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, an embedding encoder <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> selects a candidate set
<math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a reranker <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> selects the reader evidence <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{q}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\operatorname{Top}_{128}\left\{\cos(e(q),e(c)):c\in\operatorname{chunks}(H)\right\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Top</mi><mn>128</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>chunks</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\operatorname{Top}_{128}\left\{\cos(e(q),e(c)):c\in\operatorname{chunks}(H)\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S_{q}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\operatorname{Top}_{15}\left\{r(q,c):c\in D_{q}\right\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Top</mi><mn>15</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\operatorname{Top}_{15}\left\{r(q,c):c\in D_{q}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The index computes cosine similarity within the relevant
conversation. The index is fixed after ingestion, and selection uses
a fixed top-<math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> budget.
Where turn expansion is enabled,
each selected window is rendered as the complete source turns that
overlap it. Expansion changes only the reader-visible text; selection
scores remain unchanged.
Repeated turns across selected windows are included in the default
configuration. The turn-deduplication condition is a separate ablation.
Headers, overlapping content, and turn expansion determine the actual
reader context length for the 15 selected windows.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Benchmark adaptations.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">MEME presents the selected chunks in source order after relevance-based
selection to preserve temporal progression. We use the condition with
32k tokens of distractor content;
before/after questions use the evidence available at the corresponding
phase. MemoryAgentBench disables display-time turn expansion. For its
two fact-consolidation sources, each fact is treated as one message,
and the index uses one complete message per chunk with no overlap.
These atomic facts preserve their serial-number cues for conflict
resolution. Other MemoryAgentBench sources use the token-window
configuration.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">B.2. Query construction</h3>

<section id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Retrieval query.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <span id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span>, the retrieval query is the original question or user request supplied by the
benchmark: the same question text is sent to the embedding encoder and paired
with each candidate passage for reranking.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Task conditioning.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LoCoMo, LongMemEval, MEME, and PersonaMem-v2 use the original question text.
When a question date or evaluation phase is available, it determines
evidence visibility. MemoryAgentBench likewise retrieves with the original
task question; the instructions for each source are added only
when constructing the reader input.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reranker formulation.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Qwen3 reranker inserts the unchanged query and candidate passage
into a relevance-classification template:
“Given a question and a passage, determine if the passage contains
information relevant to answering the question.” A system instruction
restricts its relevance decision to yes or no.
See the full template in Appendix <a href="#A7.SS1" title="G.1. Qwen3 reranker ‣ Appendix G Prompt Templates ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reader input.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Retrieval queries are distinct from the final answer-generation prompts.
After evidence selection, the reader receives the retrieved context and
the benchmark-specific question instructions: a question-date reminder
where applicable, the appropriate MemoryAgentBench task template, or
the PersonaMem-v2 personalization reminder followed by lettered response
options.
Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Prompt Templates ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a> reproduces the reader templates and
defines all per-example substitutions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">B.3. Context-length policies</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following rules distinguish ordinary reader-input assembly from the
explicit context-budget interventions. Actual evidence-length distributions
and the resulting capped scores are reported in
Appendix <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4. Ablation studies ‣ Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Truncation and overflow.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The main <span id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> conditions have no additional evidence-token cap
and no automatic head–tail clipping after turn expansion. In all
seven examined benchmark–model conditions, the saved reader evidence
is identical to the assembled retrieval evidence, with no recorded
answer-generation error responses. The reader receives the evidence plus its task prompt and
question, and the configured output allowance is separate. The
pipeline submits the full input with that allowance. If a provider rejects
an oversized request, the default three-attempt retry policy resends the
same input. A request that exhausts these attempts is scored as an
answer-generation failure.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Other evaluated configurations have different, explicit policies.
The 100,000-token evidence cap used for SimpleMem and Memora keeps
the beginning of the assembled context in its existing order, clips
at the token boundary, and omits a truncation marker. It uses the reader’s recognized
tiktoken encoding, falling back to o200k_base;
the evidence cap excludes the question and fixed prompt.
The local Full-context condition instead budgets the assembled
request within 128,000 tokens, reserving 4,096 output tokens and a
256-token margin. When needed, it keeps approximately equal
character-length prefixes and suffixes of the history, inserts an
explicit truncation marker, and checks the resulting request length;
it fails if the prompt and question alone cannot fit.
The MEME complete-store packing rules are specified separately in
Appendix <a href="#A3.SS5" title="C.5. Complete-store and oracle evaluations ‣ Appendix C Diagnostic Procedures and Statistical Methods ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Diagnostic Procedures and Statistical Methods</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix defines the diagnostic procedures and statistical methods
used in our analyses. We formalize evidence coverage, utilization, and
executable-path retention, and describe memory-management ablations,
write-policy and context-budget interventions, and source-oracle and
complete-store controls. We also clarify
the limits of their interpretation and specify the paired statistical tests,
bootstrap confidence intervals, and multiple-comparison corrections.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">C.1. Coverage, utilization, and path retention</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For question <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and system <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{is}\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{is}\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> indicate
assessed evidence sufficiency and let <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{is}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{is}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> be its evaluated
answer score. The question-weighted summaries are</p>
<table id="A3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{C}_{s}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i}C_{is},\qquad\widehat{U}_{s}=\frac{\sum_{i}C_{is}Y_{is}}{\sum_{i}C_{is}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{C}_{s}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i}C_{is},\qquad\widehat{U}_{s}=\frac{\sum_{i}C_{is}Y_{is}}{\sum_{i}C_{is}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Utilization is undefined if no question is judged sufficient.
The product <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{C}_{s}\widehat{U}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{C}_{s}\widehat{U}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is the score contributed by
questions with sufficient evidence. Total accuracy additionally includes
<math id="A3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{-1}\sum_{i}(1-C_{is})Y_{is}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{-1}\sum_{i}(1-C_{is})Y_{is}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Correct answers outside the sufficient set may reflect sufficiency-label errors,
partial evidence, prior knowledge, or guessing. Official macro-averages
use different weights.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">MemoryAgentBench uses semantic sufficiency judgments, whereas the
MEME task-specific assessment checks required values, temporal cues,
deletion evidence, and dependency triggers. Deletion coverage requires
an explicit deletion event.
For chain-sensitive after-state questions, result coverage and
executable-path coverage are judged separately. Conditional path
retention is the fraction of result-covered questions whose context
also contains the required path. Task-specific
sufficiency rules are given below. Paired uncertainty estimates appear in
Appendix <a href="#A1.SS5.SSS1" title="A.5.1. Coverage and utilization ‣ A.5. Diagnostic analyses ‣ Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5.1</span></a>, with statistical procedures in
Appendix <a href="#A3.SS6" title="C.6. Statistical inference ‣ Appendix C Diagnostic Procedures and Statistical Methods ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Task-specific sufficiency.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">ER requires the target value; Agg all aggregated values; Tr also requires
chronology; Del-after an explicit deletion event; Cas-after the new target,
dependency rule, and upstream trigger; and Abs-after the old target,
dependency, and invalidating trigger.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">C.2. Write-policy and evidence controls</h3>

<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Mem0 mutation audit.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This analysis distinguishes emitted decisions, returned actions, and
persisted history records. Proposed mutation rates count
UPDATE and DELETE decisions relative to all emitted
decisions, including NONE. An effective update must have a
persisted record with available old and new text that differ; a
persisted deletion also counts as effective mutation. Effectiveness is
defined by persisted text changes. In the ADD/NONE-only intervention, proposed
updates become additions and proposed deletions become no-ops.
Figure <a href="#S0.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> orders all 500 LongMemEval conversations by
proposed mutation rate, pairing each proposed rate (orange) with its
effective rate (blue).
The experiment tests the usefulness of that mutation policy for
downstream QA.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evidence interventions.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The candidate sets, selection rules, packing budgets, and reader instructions
for the answer-location source oracle and gold-informed complete-store
diagnostic are specified below.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">C.3. Memory-management ablations</h3>

<div id="A3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The three memory-management variants in Table <a href="#S4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 4.3. Ablation studies and efficiency ‣ 4. RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> reuse the
<span id="A3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> retrieval and reader stack, changing the representation built
at ingestion or the candidate set passed to reranking. Unless stated
otherwise, all conditions tokenize each conversation into 256-token
windows with 32-token overlap, embed the resulting items with
text-embedding-3-small, retrieve the 128 highest cosine-similarity
candidates, rerank those candidates with the same cross-encoder, and pass
the final 15 items to the reader. All LLM calls for these operations use
gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18, temperature zero, and a JSON object
containing only the requested fields; malformed or empty outputs are
retried, and repeated failure aborts the build.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Atomic facts.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each raw window, the extractor returns at most six standalone facts.
Each fact contains the fact text, speaker, time, within-window order,
serial/newness marker, up to five entities, and a short supporting quote.
Facts are restricted to information explicitly supported by the window;
names, dates, speaker labels, event order, and serial numbers are preserved.
Each fact is capped at 28 words and each source quote at 12 words.
The raw window is then replaced in the searchable memory index by one
retrieval item per extracted fact. Each item retains the source-window
identifier and token span, and is rendered with the fact fields and
provenance before embedding. Thus, the atomic-fact condition does not
present the original window as reader evidence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Raw summary.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each raw window, a companion summary is generated with five fields:
a dense factual summary, ordered key events, important entities, temporal
or serial-order cues, and conflicts or updates. The searchable item
contains both the complete raw window and this structured summary, so the
summary augments rather than replaces the source text. The item retains
the source-window identifier and token span. This condition therefore
changes the indexed text and its embedding while preserving the raw
evidence in each retrieved item.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Graph-expanded raw.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The graph nodes are the original raw windows. During ingestion, each new
window is compared by embedding similarity with up to its ten nearest
earlier windows. An LLM then selects any genuinely related candidates and
returns a short reason for each accepted link. Links are accepted only
from this candidate set and are stored as undirected edges, even though
they are created from an earlier window to the new window. At query time,
the question embedding first selects 32 seed windows. The candidate set is
expanded by adding every one-hop graph neighbor of each seed; no positive
candidate cap is applied in the evaluated configuration. The expanded raw
windows, rather than graph descriptions, are then reranked and the top 15
are shown to the reader. Graph cues are disabled in the reader context.
Thus, graph expansion changes candidate recall but preserves reader-visible source text.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Shared configuration.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The three variants use separate local, conversation-scoped indices.
Ingestion-time memory management is concurrent, with configured maxima of 80, 16, and
32 concurrent calls for atomic facts, raw summaries, and graph-expanded
raw, respectively. Embeddings are requested in batches of 128. The
MemoryAgentBench condition disables display-time turn expansion and treats
each prepared fact as a complete message with no overlap; other benchmark
conditions retain the ordinary display-time policy. The exact prompts and
JSON schemas are reproduced in the prompt appendix,
Appendix <a href="#A7.SS2" title="G.2. Memory-management variants ‣ Appendix G Prompt Templates ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">C.4. Context-budget interventions</h3>

<div id="A3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The two 5k interventions start from the original retrieved items and
alter only their display and packing.
The outer prompt and question are outside this budget.
Appendix <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4. Ablation studies ‣ Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a> reports the results for each
display policy and set of systems.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Prefix-based truncation.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This separate control freezes the retrieved evidence and applies a
5,000-token ceiling to the formatted evidence, including its headers
and separators, using o200k_base. It preserves the longest
<em id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">prefix of complete ranked entries</em> that fits. At the first
non-fitting entry it stops.
If the first entry alone is too long, it keeps that entry’s first
5,000 tokens. This is the sole partial-entry case. The resulting prefix
is passed to the reader without a truncation marker, with any remaining
budget unused.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Expansion-aware budgeting.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We additionally freeze the selected top-15 chunks and their ordering
for the main GPT-4o-mini LoCoMo and MEME conditions and the
GPT-4.1-mini LongMemEval condition. Evidence already within 5,000
o200k_base tokens is left verbatim. For each over-budget
question, we first disable turn expansion for <em id="A3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">all</em> selected
chunks and display their original token-window text, including
headers and separators. If this still exceeds the ceiling, the
fallback is the longest prefix of complete entries in the existing
display order, with token-prefix clipping only when the first entry
alone exceeds the budget. This fallback uses the same prefix rule as
the six-system intervention, including omission of a truncation marker.
MEME uses chronological order; LoCoMo and LongMemEval use
relevance order. No deduplication or task-specific readout instruction
is added, and the ceiling excludes the outer prompt and question.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">C.5. Complete-store and oracle evaluations</h3>

<div id="A3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following diagnostic conditions provide source-oracle evidence or gold-informed
access to the complete store and its raw control.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Answer-location source oracle.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The source oracle in Table <a href="#S1.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> supplies all annotated
supporting sessions at or before the question’s cutoff, in chronological
order. Selection uses neither the question, gold answer, nor entity values
to choose among those sessions, and applies no token cap or truncation.
Non-Abs tasks use the standard reader prompt; Abs alone uses the value-free
dependency-validity instruction in Appendix <a href="#A7.SS11" title="G.11. Dependency-validity control ‣ Appendix G Prompt Templates ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.11</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Complete-store access.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We denote native retrieval by <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, the complete-store condition by <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>,
and its raw-evidence control by <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>. The complete-store condition replaces
native retrieval with gold-informed selection over exported persisted
representations. Its raw control filters
phase-visible annotated source sessions using the same gold fields.
Both pack evidence within a nominal 5,000-token context budget and append
the task policy in Appendix <a href="#A7.SS12" title="G.12. Complete-store task policy ‣ Appendix G Prompt Templates ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.12</span></a>.
That policy supplies phase, task type, target identifiers, and transition
semantics to every task. In particular, its after-phase Abs branch states
that the upstream entity changed without a replacement target.
The policy is value-free and supplies this task/status information explicitly.
Compared with the answer-location oracle, selection, context assembly,
budget, and readout instructions all change.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Gold-informed selection.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The selector matches each question to its annotated task and collects
target-entity before values, plus after values and history for after-phase
questions; it also collects before/after values of a cascade source
when present, the question’s annotated entity values, the expected
answer, and target/cascade entity names with underscores replaced
by spaces. Strings are lowercased, normalized to word sequences,
and deduplicated. For a stored object <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(m)</annotation></semantics></math> be the set
of normalized phrases occurring as substrings and let <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> be the
question’s word set. The memory-object ranking is</p>
<table id="A3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(m)=\sum_{h\in H(m)}\left(4+\min(4,|h|/3)\right)+2\frac{|\operatorname{words}(m)\cap Q|}{\max(1,|Q|)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>words</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(m)=\sum_{h\in H(m)}\left(4+\min(4,|h|/3)\right)+2\frac{|\operatorname{words}(m)\cap Q|}{\max(1,|Q|)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|h|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|h|</annotation></semantics></math> counts words. No retrieval-similarity term contributes
to this complete-store ranking. Objects without a phrase hit or
query-word overlap are not selected; ties in descending score are
resolved by the order in which objects were enumerated from the store.
Gold values and answers guide this offline selector only. The reader’s
context contains the selected memory text and the task policy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Temporal eligibility and provenance.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">“Complete” refers to enumerating persisted reader-visible
representations. Table <a href="#A3.T33" title="Table 33 ‣ Temporal eligibility and provenance. ‣ C.5. Complete-store and oracle evaluations ‣ Appendix C Diagnostic Procedures and Statistical Methods ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a> specifies each export.
For provenance-filtered stores, a candidate must have a nonempty
mapped source-session set whose maximum index does not exceed the
question cutoff. Normalized duplicate texts are removed, keeping the
first exported occurrence. EMem instead uses its actual before/after
snapshot and deduplicates within that snapshot. Raw provenance text
is used for provenance checks or session mapping, never substituted for an
extracted memory. Exported model-control-token literals are escaped as text.</p>
