<section id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Snapshot limitation.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Except for EMem’s explicit phase snapshots, exports reflect the final
store and are filtered using source metadata. Overwritten or deleted
objects are unavailable, and surviving objects may contain later revisions.
Mem0 is particularly subject to this limitation because it maps object
creation time.
Missing or unmappable required provenance invalidates an export
unless an explicit conservative rule applies; the
MemMachine rule above is an explicit exception for uncited
consolidated objects. “Phase-visible” refers to visibility under these
export and filtering rules.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Ordering and truncation.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The diagnostic uses o200k_base token counting and reserves
the task-policy tokens before evidence packing. The nominal 5,000-token
budget covers formatted evidence and policy; the outer reader prompt and
question are excluded, and the joining newline is added afterward. Token
counts refer to the formatted context actually supplied to the reader.
The following packing rules are part of the reported conditions:</p>
<ol id="A3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ordinary memory objects.</span> Mem0, MemOS, MemU,
EverMemOS, and MemMachine present separately delimited memory blocks in
gold-relevance order. If the first block
exceeds the available evidence budget, retain its token prefix;
subsequently skip any non-fitting block and continue to later
candidates. Headers count toward the budget.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RAG whole chunks.</span> Add one point to <math id="A3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(m)</annotation></semantics></math> when a
chunk overlaps an annotated phase-visible evidence session, and
admit location-matching chunks even without lexical hits.
Greedily select fitting chunks by new phrase coverage, then score,
then enumeration order; finally display them in source-text order.
Skip oversized chunks without truncating them.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EMem.</span> Agg and Del use the ordinary relevance-order
packer. ER keeps the full ranked list but packs whole EDUs and then
restores chronology. Tr, Cas, and Abs first select a compact set
covering each available gold-value phrase once, add at most two
target/cascade-label EDUs, and fall back to the first three ranked
EDUs if none were selected. Pack fitting whole EDUs, then sort by
earliest source session and enumeration order; EDUs without
session provenance appear last. This chronological packer skips
non-fitting EDUs rather than truncating them.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Raw control.</span> Among annotated sessions at or before
the cutoff, retain sessions with at least one gold-phrase hit;
if none match, fall back to all visible annotated sessions.
Display selected sessions chronologically. Truncate an oversized
first session to the evidence budget; skip later non-fitting
sessions and continue. This differs both from untruncated
answer-location readout and from RAG’s indivisible chunk packing.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px7" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Budget relaxation.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The no-cap intervention removes only the 5,000-token stopping condition from
the packers above. It preserves each condition’s exported candidate set,
gold-informed relevance scores, admission rules, display order, task policy,
reader model, and scoring procedure. Thus every admitted candidate appears
in the same final display order; the raw control is unchanged because all of
its selected evidence already fits. Reader inputs that are byte-identical to
the 5,000-token condition reuse the corresponding answer and judgment; changed
inputs are answered with the same GPT-4o-mini snapshot, and identical answer
strings reuse judgments. Before-phase gates are recomputed over the full
paired set before after-phase task scores and affected-subset scores are
formed. The resulting contrasts are reported in
Table <a href="#A1.T30" title="Table 30 ‣ Packing-cap sensitivity. ‣ A.5.2. Complete-store evaluation ‣ A.5. Diagnostic analyses ‣ Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30</span></a>.
All accepted new reader responses identify the GPT-4o-mini-2024-07-18
snapshot; responses identifying a different model are discarded and
the original requests are retried. Each accepted request is checked
against its prepared input fingerprint, and its server-reported prompt
token count must match the full prepared prompt. No condition exceeds
the context window. For MemMachine, 16 unchanged inputs reuse historical
results and 1,172 inputs receive new reader responses. Intervals use
20,000 paired episode-bootstrap resamples, conditional on the observed
outputs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px8" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Interpretive limitations.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The raw-to-store gap reflects both stored information and the process used
to present it to the reader. Export coverage and temporal provenance define
the candidate evidence; selection, ordering, packing, and reading determine
how that evidence is used. The comparison therefore does not isolate
information loss during ingestion. Native-to-complete gains likewise combine
broader access to stored evidence with changes to task information and
readout policy. Relations missed by native retrieval may remain in the store.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px8.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS5.SSS0.Px8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Task scores in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS5.SSS2" title="A.5.2. Complete-store evaluation ‣ A.5. Diagnostic analyses ‣ Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5.2</span></a> show where the
aggregate comparison needs care: raw evidence scores below 100%,
and system rankings vary by task. On MEME Abs, missing evidence
can elicit an “uncertain” answer that receives credit, so a correct answer
does not necessarily indicate successful dependency reconstruction.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">C.6. Statistical inference</h3>

<div id="A3.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All procedures below operate on paired outcomes from the stated completed
conditions. The resampling unit follows the benchmark structure and keeps
repeated judgments of each answer together. Reported intervals cover
question/episode sampling uncertainty conditional on the observed reader
and semantic-judge outputs, store builds, and sufficiency labels, including those
from the deterministic MEME verifier.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Coverage and utilization.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A1.T25" title="Table 25 ‣ A.5.1. Coverage and utilization ‣ A.5. Diagnostic analyses ‣ Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a> gives the four omnibus tests underlying
Section <a href="#S5" title="5. Why Is Raw-RAG Enough in Most Cases? ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. MemoryAgentBench coverage uses Cochran’s <math id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> on
the seven paired binary sufficiency outcomes, followed by exact paired McNemar
tests. Its intervals use 50,000 source-stratified paired bootstrap samples.
Because utilization has a system-specific denominator, its omnibus and
pairwise tests instead use a centered max-range bootstrap and two-sided
bootstrap sign tests. The MemoryAgentBench resampling shares question indices
across systems within each of the 14 sources. The MEME analyses resample 100
whole episodes with shared indices across systems, including all questions and
before/after pairs from each sampled episode. They use 50,000 replicates. In
every case, Holm adjustment is applied over the 21 pairwise comparisons within
one benchmark–metric family.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Retrieval-design ablations.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The MemoryAgentBench design ablations use 20,000 paired,
source-stratified bootstrap resamples and Holm correction, as reported
in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. Ablation studies and efficiency ‣ 4. RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>. Source scores are recomputed under the
benchmark’s capability-weighting scheme rather than pooling all questions.
Final-<math id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> intervals compare each condition with the default <math id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=15</annotation></semantics></math>;
capability-specific contrasts use their stated task family.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Complete-store contrasts.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each contrast, 20,000 paired bootstrap replicates sample 100
episodes with replacement, keeping all questions and both phases
of an episode together. Each replicate recomputes the six-task
macro-average in both conditions and then their difference; the
2.5th and 97.5th percentiles give the interval. The <math id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R-C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R-C</annotation></semantics></math> intervals
use the same after-phase scores as Figure <a href="#S5.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ 5.2. Raw history preserves the paths needed for reasoning ‣ 5. Why Is Raw-RAG Enough in Most Cases? ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.
The <math id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C-N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C-N</annotation></semantics></math> intervals
use those same complete-store scores and the aligned Native scores.
These are pointwise intervals, without multiplicity correction,
conditional on the observed reader and judge
outputs and store builds. RAG Native aggregates three judge calls,
while its complete-store and raw controls use one.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Question identities and after-phase scores are aligned across the
compared conditions. The original random seeds for the <math id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R-C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R-C</annotation></semantics></math>
bootstrap are unavailable, so new resamples may yield slightly different
interval endpoints.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Experimental Implementation and Reproducibility</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix specifies the experimental settings needed to reproduce
our evaluations, including benchmark inputs and task definitions,
baseline memory construction and retrieval configurations, and shared
evaluation procedures. We also document scoring and averaging rules,
timing and readiness criteria, and the software and hardware environment.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">D.1. Benchmark descriptions</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Alongside the four main benchmarks, we describe PersonaMem-v2 for the
supplementary accuracy comparison and BEAM for ingestion timing only.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic ltx_title_subsubsection">D.1.1. LoCoMo</h4>

<section id="A4.SS1.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Data and tasks.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LoCoMo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> supplies long, multi-session
conversations between two named speakers, with session dates, dialogue
identifiers, and, for some turns, images and textual captions. A question
refers to the history of one conversation and requests a free-form answer.
We use the 10-conversation release, with 1,540 questions after
excluding adversarial category 5. The remaining types are single-hop
(category 4, 841 questions), multi-hop (category 1, 282), temporal
(category 2, 321), and open-domain (category 3, 96).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Capability requirements.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Single-hop questions test precise recovery of an entity or fact from a
particular interaction. Multi-hop questions require combining facts
distributed across turns or sessions, including resolving who said or
experienced what. Temporal questions require anchoring relative dates
to the conversation timeline and reasoning about event order.
Open-domain questions connect conversational evidence with broader
knowledge or reasonable inference. The shared difficulty is finding
sparse evidence in a long, personally grounded history while preserving
speaker attribution, names, and time references.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS1.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation setup.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each dialogue history forms one memory store shared by its questions.
Session boundaries and speaker attribution are preserved.
Images are represented by the supplied caption in message
text. All included questions reuse their
conversation’s memory store; question category and reference evidence
are reserved for evaluation. The
different published judge and lexical-metric conventions remain separated
in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic ltx_title_subsubsection">D.1.2. LongMemEval-S</h4>

<section id="A4.SS1.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Data and tasks.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each LongMemEval-S example <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> contains a
user–assistant question, its question date, and a dated collection of
historical chat sessions containing both relevant and distractor
interactions. Our standalone evaluation uses 500 examples. Its six
reported question types are <em id="A4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">single-session-user</em> (recall a
user-provided fact), <em id="A4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">single-session-assistant</em> (recall an
assistant-provided fact), <em id="A4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">single-session-preference</em> (use a
preference expressed within a session), <em id="A4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">multi-session</em>
(combine evidence across sessions), <em id="A4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">knowledge-update</em>
(select the valid information after a change), and <em id="A4.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">temporal-reasoning</em>
(reason about dates, intervals, or order). Unanswerable/abstention
examples form an additional evaluation condition across these types.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Capability requirements.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike LoCoMo, where multiple questions share a small fixed set of histories,
LongMemEval supplies a separate conversation history for each example. The benchmark tests
whether a system can distinguish user from assistant knowledge, recover
preferences, integrate dispersed evidence, resolve superseded facts,
and avoid answering when the history lacks support. The question date
is the reference time for phrases such as “current” or “last month”.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS2.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation setup.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS2.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS2.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each example has an isolated memory store that preserves session dates
and speaker roles. Annotated answer sessions are used only for
evaluation. The reader receives the question-date instruction
shown in Appendix <a href="#A7.SS5" title="G.5. LongMemEval reader ‣ Appendix G Prompt Templates ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.5</span></a>. Published
non-abstention scores use their specified question subsets;
our full-set scores use all 500 examples.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic ltx_title_subsubsection">D.1.3. MEME</h4>

<section id="A4.SS1.SSS3.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Data and tasks.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS3.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS3.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">MEME <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> supplies chronological episodes in personal-life
and software-project domains. Each contains evidence sessions,
distractor sessions, entity values, and explicit dependency rules,
with questions before and after a designated change. We use the
condition with 32k tokens of distractor content: 100 episodes and 1,188 phase-specific
questions. Its six types distinguish different operations:</p>
<ul id="A4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exact Recall (ER):</span> reproduce a stable entity value.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Aggregation (Agg):</span> collect several entities’ required
values from distributed evidence.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tracking (Tr):</span> reconstruct an entity’s sequence of
historical values in chronological order.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Deletion (Del):</span> acknowledge removal without revealing
the deleted value.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cascade (Cas):</span> apply a stated conditional rule after
an upstream change to derive a dependent entity’s new value,
including one- and two-hop dependencies.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Absence (Abs):</span> recognize that a dependent old value
is no longer trustworthy when an upstream condition changes and
no applicable replacement value is supplied.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS3.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Capability requirements.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS3.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fictitious values reduce the usefulness of parametric factual knowledge.
The task crosses single- versus multi-entity reasoning with static
versus evolving information. In particular, Cas requires executing an
update rule, whereas Abs requires withholding an unsupported current
value. Del evaluates whether the answer respects a removal request;
physical erasure from storage is outside its scoring criteria.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS3.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation setup.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS3.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS3.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Questions carry their episode, before/after phase, target entities,
dependency depth, and temporal cutoff. All systems receive the official
short-answer prompt. Scoring uses task-specific judgments:
Tr checks the ordered history, Agg checks the required
values, and Cas, Abs, and Del have different correctness conditions.
Task scores use after-phase answers. For Cas, Abs, and Del, an
after-phase pass receives credit only if the corresponding before-phase
answer was also correct, preventing a system that never knew a value
from receiving credit merely for failing to report it. Overall is the
six-task macro-average.
Phase visibility is a requirement of each run; retrieval-time timestamp
filtering and the explicit phase-scoped oracle controls are distinguished
below and in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Raw-RAG: Method Details ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic ltx_title_subsubsection">D.1.4. MemoryAgentBench</h4>

<section id="A4.SS1.SSS4.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Data and tasks.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS4.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS4.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">MemoryAgentBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> presents a long source
context as a sequence of memory-ingestion units, followed by multiple
questions over the same context. The evaluated suite has 2,071
questions from 14 sources, organized into four capabilities:</p>
<ul id="A4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Accurate Retrieval (AR):</span> LongMemEval chat-history
questions, EventQA next-event questions over narrative evidence,
and the two RULER QA sources over long document collections.
These test locating the correct conversational fact, event, or
document-supported answer amid distractors.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Test-Time Learning (TTL):</span> five in-context classification
sources (Banking77, CLINC150, NLU, TREC-coarse, and TREC-fine)
supply labeled demonstrations and ask for a numerical label for a
new input. ReDial supplies recommendation dialogues and asks for
movie recommendations. These test remembering and applying examples
or preferences acquired during ingestion, with model weights fixed.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Long-Range Understanding (LRU):</span> DetectiveQA asks for
answers requiring narrative-level reasoning, while InfBench asks
for book summarization. These test integrating globally distributed
clues or salient content.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conflict Resolution (CR):</span> single-hop and multi-hop
FactConsolidation supply numbered, potentially conflicting facts.
A larger serial number denotes newer information. Questions require
selecting the latest valid fact and, for multi-hop cases, composing
relations under that rule instead of relying on real-world knowledge.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS4.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Capability requirements.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS4.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS4.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The “ingest once, query many” structure measures how a reusable
memory representation supports heterogeneous downstream tasks. Exact
example-to-label associations matter for TTL; narrative coverage
matters for LRU; provenance and precedence matter for CR. Compression
that benefits one capability can remove precisely the information
needed by another.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS4.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation setup.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS4.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS4.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each source context forms one shared memory store, using that source’s
ingestion units and question wording. Splitting the source into ingestion
units is distinct from the subsequent retrieval-window construction
in <span id="A4.SS1.SSS4.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span>. Original LongMemEval session dates
are preserved where available. For otherwise undated sources,
assigned timestamps encode source order;
FactConsolidation precedence comes from its original serial numbers.
Scoring follows each source’s task: substring matching, numerical-label
or choice accuracy, recommendation recall, or LLM-based judging and
summary scoring. The aggregate combines these native task metrics using
the nested capability weights specified below. The embedded LongMemEval
source uses separate evaluation settings from the standalone
500-example benchmark.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic ltx_title_subsubsection">D.1.5. PersonaMem-v2</h4>

<section id="A4.SS1.SSS5.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Data and tasks.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS5.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS5.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our supplementary evaluation uses the PersonaMem-v2 text
benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> and 32k chat
histories, with 5,000 multiple-choice questions. Each input pairs a
personalized user request with candidate responses. The nine scenario
types are consultation, personal email, translation, professional email,
creative writing, professional writing, knowledge query, social media,
and chat.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS5.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Capability requirements.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS5.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS5.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The task asks whether a response fits the user’s preferences and
circumstances from past interactions. Preference attribution and updates
can determine which plausible response is appropriate,
testing personalization grounded in conversational evidence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS5.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation setup.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS5.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS5.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Questions referring to the same 32k history share a memory store.
System-role persona messages are excluded from the ingested dialogue;
user and assistant turns are included.
The persona name can appear as a speaker label; full persona
profiles are excluded from <span id="A4.SS1.SSS5.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span>’s memory evidence.
The reader receives the question and lettered response options;
accuracy is exact agreement between the selected and reference letters.
Preference annotations and reference snippets are reserved for evaluation.
Option order and the corresponding answer letters must remain fixed
across compared runs. Table <a href="#A1.T18" title="Table 18 ‣ Experimental configuration. ‣ A.3. PersonaMem-v2 results ‣ Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a> reports the results
under this multiple-choice evaluation condition.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS6" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic ltx_title_subsubsection">D.1.6. BEAM ingestion workload</h4>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">BEAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> supplies the long conversation used for
the scaling study. Our input is one ordered, 1,710-message conversation
approaching one million tokens. Each message is added before
the next timed Add operation begins. We use BEAM solely to measure ingestion
throughput, readiness cost, and persistence under a growing history.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">D.2. Evaluation workflow</h3>

<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each experiment separates four operations: ingesting the available
history, retrieving evidence for a question, generating an answer,
and scoring that answer. Histories preserve speaker attribution and
available dates. Questions and reference answers remain separate from
memory construction; references are used only for evaluation and the
explicitly identified diagnostic controls. Each system uses its own
memory construction and retrieval procedure. The selected evidence is
then presented to a reader under the stated prompt condition
and scored according to the benchmark.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The configurations below specify system-specific memory units, speaker
scope, and retrieval budgets; comparison scope is summarized in
Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Temporal visibility.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Where supported, retrieval uses the question date to exclude later
memories. Several systems first retrieve a larger candidate set, filter
it by timestamp, and then apply the final evidence budget; memories
with unknown timestamps are kept by default.
A transformed item can combine evidence from multiple times.
EMem has explicit question-date-scoped indexing for applicable
datasets, while oracle analyses use their specified phase-visible
sources or snapshots. Timestamp filtering alone does not establish temporal
validity for revised memories or undated semantic features; their visibility
also depends on how each system constructs and updates its store.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">D.3. Baseline configurations</h3>

<div id="A4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The configurations below specify each agentic memory system’s
memory-management operations and ordinary execution settings, alongside
the <span id="A4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> and Full-context reference conditions.
Concurrency denotes a configured upper bound. Conversation-level
ingestion, concurrent queries, and internal model calls are different
levels of parallelism. Benchmark-specific conditions are identified
separately; in particular, the timing experiments use the single-worker
setup described below. The shared answer stage defaults to 50
concurrent requests unless a method or run overrides it.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic ltx_title_subsubsection">D.3.1. <span id="A4.SS3.SSS1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span></h4>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The source-preserving representation, retrieval and reranking procedure,
benchmark-specific evidence assembly, and query construction are defined
in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Raw-RAG: Method Details ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. Ablation conditions change only their stated
operation while keeping the corresponding benchmark inputs and scoring
procedure. Here we specify the execution configuration.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Execution settings.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The default limits are two concurrent
conversations during indexing, eight search requests, and 50 answer
requests. Embeddings are requested in batches of 128. The default
store is a local index maintained separately for each conversation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic ltx_title_subsubsection">D.3.2. EverMemOS</h4>

<section id="A4.SS3.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Memory construction.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">EverMemOS constructs organized episodic memories and associated
structured information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. Histories are segmented
into coherent memory units called MemCells, from which episodic
representations and atomic facts are extracted. Lexical and embedding
indices support retrieval. We also enable incremental profile extraction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Retrieval and readout.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Retrieval combines lexical and semantic search with query refinement
and checks for evidence sufficiency. By default, 10 results are
returned and presented as subject–episode blocks.
Answer generation follows the system’s own procedure,
with benchmark-specific prompts where specified.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS2.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Execution settings.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">EverMemOS runs locally.
Its extraction concurrency is 20, with a separate
atomic-fact concurrency limit of 20. Index construction permits five
concurrent embedding batches. Agentic retrieval
limits concurrent questions to 20, and its native response stage
allows 50 concurrent requests.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic ltx_title_subsubsection">D.3.3. MemOS</h4>

<section id="A4.SS3.SSS3.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Memory construction.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS3.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS3.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">MemOS manages extracted text memories and preference information
within its memory operating-system abstraction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>.
We use its fine-grained extraction setting and wait for each ingestion
step to finish before proceeding. Memories are extracted and stored
by MemOS itself.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS3.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Retrieval and readout.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS3.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Retrieval requests the top 20 text memories and preferences using
the fine search mode, reranking, and maximal-marginal-relevance
deduplication. We choose fine rather than the default
fast mode to evaluate MemOS’s memory-management capabilities.
For two named conversational speakers, the system writes
and searches separate speaker perspectives and formats both sets of
memories, including returned preferences, for the reader. In
MemoryAgentBench it instead uses a context-scoped identity. For its
ICL sources, the reader additionally receives up to five original
label-mapping chunks selected by lexical overlap with the question.
These chunks supply raw labeled demonstrations from the ingested context.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS3.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Execution settings.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS3.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use locally hosted MemOS 2.0.23. Ordinary
evaluation allows 10 conversation workers and 10 concurrent searches.
Add batches contain 10 messages, reduced to six for LongMemEval and
two for MemoryAgentBench, with a 0.1-second request interval.
Storage uses Qdrant 1.15.3 and Neo4j, with version 5.26.6 specified
and version 5.26.4 permitted as a fallback.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic ltx_title_subsubsection">D.3.4. EMem and EMem-G</h4>

<section id="A4.SS3.SSS4.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Memory construction.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS4.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS4.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">EMem represents conversations using extracted elementary discourse
units (EDUs); EMem-G additionally uses graph-based
retrieval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>. We use contextual extraction of EDUs
without a further context-generation step. Session and turn structure
is preserved. Where question dates define different visible histories,
separate stores can be built from the corresponding history prefixes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS4.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Retrieval and readout.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS4.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS4.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">EMem retrieves without personalized PageRank, whereas EMem-G uses it
to propagate relevance through the graph; the two variants are distinct.
The default QA budget is 10, overridden to 20 for MEME and
MemoryAgentBench. Retrieved EDUs, or session documents when EDUs are
unavailable, provide the reader evidence.
For ICL it also prepends up to five lexical-overlap-selected raw
demonstration chunks, as in MemOS. MEME and MemoryAgentBench can use
the aligned benchmark reader over this evidence; other conditions
use EMem’s own answer-generation procedure. These answer conditions
are distinguished in the experiment records.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS4.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Execution settings.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS4.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS4.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">EMem processes conversations and date-scoped histories sequentially
with internal batched model calls; it allows up to
50 in-flight requests. Embedding batches contain up to 256 items,
and answer generation uses up to 32 workers.
The random seed is 42. Extracted memories and indices are reused
only when inputs and experimental settings match.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic ltx_title_subsubsection">D.3.5. SimpleMem</h4>

<section id="A4.SS3.SSS5.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Memory construction.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS5.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS5.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SimpleMem converts dialogue windows into compact, structured memory
entries and combines several retrieval channels <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>.
Our direct evaluation covers LoCoMo. Each conversation has a separate
memory store, built from timestamped windows of 40 messages with a
two-message overlap. The final partial window is also included.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS5.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Retrieval and readout.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS5.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS5.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Retrieval combines semantic, keyword, and
structured retrieval, with configured channel budgets of 25, 5, and
5, planning enabled, and up to two reflection rounds. We preserve
the merged native order and pass at most 20 entries to the reader,
using each entry’s factual restatement and timestamp. Embeddings
follow the experiment’s stated backbone. Final answering uses the
shared benchmark reader and prompt. The published SimpleMem
LongMemEval result is attributed to its source separately.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS5.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Execution settings.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS5.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS5.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Two conversations may be ingested concurrently, and search uses four
query workers. Within a query, parallel retrieval is enabled with
up to three channel workers. Each conversation has its own LanceDB
store. Search has a 600-second timeout and two configured attempts;
the shared reader has a separate 100,000-token context cap.
These settings apply to our LoCoMo evaluation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS6" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic ltx_title_subsubsection">D.3.6. MemU</h4>

<section id="A4.SS3.SSS6.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Memory construction.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS6.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS6.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">MemU extracts profile, event, knowledge, and behavior items from
interaction resources <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>. Each conversation is
treated as a separate user’s history, and its messages are ingested
in order.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS6.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Retrieval and readout.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS6.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS6.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the default configuration of MemU 1.5.1.
Retrieval stays within the same conversation and includes intention
routing and evidence-sufficiency checks. Retrieved items are supplied to
the shared reader. The final result cap is 20 by default and 10 for
MemoryAgentBench; internal candidate retrieval has a separate budget.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS6.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Execution settings.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS6.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS6.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use MemU 1.5.1. Ingestion permits five
concurrent conversations but preserves the order of writes within
each conversation. Ingestion batches contain up to 128 messages, with
one message per MemU resource. Persistence uses PostgreSQL with
pgvector. Search batches up to eight questions at a time and reuses
a conversation-scoped GPU item index for exact cosine similarity
on the GPU; index loading fetches 512 items per batch.
GPU similarity computation is separate from API-based embeddings
and LLM routing and sufficiency checks.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS7" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic ltx_title_subsubsection">D.3.7. MemMachine</h4>

<section id="A4.SS3.SSS7.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Memory construction.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS7.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS7.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">MemMachine combines source-grounded episodic memory with extracted
semantic memory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>.
Each conversation has an isolated store with both memory types
enabled. Semantic extraction may continue after message ingestion,
so ingestion completion and readiness for retrieval are distinct
timing boundaries.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS7.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Retrieval and readout.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS7.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS7.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the default configuration of MemMachine 0.3.4.
Episodes, episode summaries, and semantic features appear in separate
context sections. Episodic and semantic lists have
independent top-20 budgets and keep their separate rankings. Episodic
candidates are date-filtered, but undated semantic features bypass the
filter. The date cutoff therefore does not establish temporal validity
for semantic evidence. The formatted sections are sent to the
shared reader for final answer generation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS7.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Execution settings.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS7.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS7.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use MemMachine 0.3.4 with model access through OpenRouter.
Storage uses PostgreSQL 16 with pgvector and
Neo4j 5.23 Community. Ordinary evaluation configures 10 ingestion
workers and 10 search workers, with 10-message Add batches
(two for MemoryAgentBench). Ordinary evaluation uses a
1,800-second post-Add wait. Timing runs instead wait for semantic
ingestion to finish.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS8" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic ltx_title_subsubsection">D.3.8. Mem0</h4>

<section id="A4.SS3.SSS8.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Memory management.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS8.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS8.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Classic Mem0 extracts candidate facts,
retrieves related existing memories, and chooses ADD, UPDATE, DELETE,
or NONE operations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. Message batches are ingested
chronologically into separate conversation stores, with memory changes
recorded for the mutation audit. This also defines the unmodified-policy control.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS8.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Retrieval and readout.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS8.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS8.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the default configuration of each evaluated Mem0 version.
For two named speakers, the system maintains separate
perspectives and queries both; MemoryAgentBench instead uses one
context-scoped identity. Native memory search supplies the text
facts, with a configured top-20 budget per searched identity.
Retrieved facts undergo temporal filtering
before being supplied to the shared reader. The extraction model
is selected separately from the answer model; the default reader
uses the structured reasoning prompt in Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Prompt Templates ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>.
The ADD/NONE-only policy converts proposed UPDATE to ADD and DELETE to NONE.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS8.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Execution settings.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS8.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS8.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The general local evaluation uses Mem0 2.0.11, including the
MEME store used in the evidence analyses, whereas the classic
mutation audit and ADD/NONE intervention use Mem0 1.0.11.
Ordinary evaluation permits five
conversation workers and five search workers, with five-message
Add batches (two for MemoryAgentBench) and an 8,000-character
per-message limit. Writes remain sequential within a conversation;
the sharded configuration uses separate on-disk Qdrant and SQLite
files per conversation to avoid concurrent-writer conflicts.
Model requests have a 60-second timeout and no HTTP-client retries.
Failed ingestion stages can be retried, and timeout-triggered
splitting can further subdivide a message batch.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS9" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic ltx_title_subsubsection">D.3.9. Memora</h4>

<section id="A4.SS3.SSS9.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Memory construction.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS9.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS9.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Memora separates stored memory values from indexed primary abstractions
and cue anchors, linking compact retrieval representations to more
specific factual and episodic content <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>.
Each conversation has an isolated store. Messages are grouped by
session and segmented before memory extraction, with source
attribution preserved. MemoryAgentBench skips this extra segmentation
and ingests its prepared source chunks directly.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS9.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Retrieval and readout.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS9.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS9.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Search uses a prompted-policy retriever with at most five steps.
The evaluated configuration
enables cue indexing, BM25 hybrid search, and episodic memory, with
top-20 output before applicable temporal processing. For each
returned factual memory, the reader receives its value and history
together with linked episodic memories, supplying both specific
conversation evidence and factual details to the common reader.
The answer context has a separate 100,000-token cap. This condition
combines Memora’s memory-management pipeline with a shared reader;
the published policy-retriever scores are attributed separately.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS9.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Execution settings.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS9.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS9.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Ordinary evaluation permits 10 ingestion workers and 10 search workers,
with a separate limit of two segmentation workers; resumed runs can
reduce conversation concurrency. Each conversation uses an isolated
Chroma persistent directory. LLM-based segmentation determines the
ordinary ingestion units; the configured two-message batch size
applies to non-LLM segmentation. MemoryAgentBench disables the extra
segmentation and ingests one prepared source chunk at a time.
Extraction inputs are bounded to 10,000 characters and can be
recursively split down to 1,000 characters after output-length
failures. The model-request timeout
is 600 seconds with three retries; extraction uses seed 42
and zero temperature.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS10" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic ltx_title_subsubsection">D.3.10. Full-context</h4>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS10.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Full-context omits both memory transformation and retrieval: the
reader receives the visible chronological history directly, filtered
by question date when supplied, under the bounded-history policy in
Appendix <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3. Context-length policies ‣ Appendix B Raw-RAG: Method Details ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>.
Published Full-context results retain their original settings.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS10.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Execution settings.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS10.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS10.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">No memory transformation or vector index is used.
History preparation uses four workers
and 10 answer workers. These limits and the context truncation rule
apply only to our direct evaluation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">D.4. Experimental controls</h3>

<div id="A4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We save the reader context, answer, and score for each question to
distinguish retrieval, generation, and judging changes under the
conditions defined in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation comparability.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A complete evaluation requires the expected question count and a unique
identifier linking each question to its context, answer, and judgments
within a source. For MEME, evidence must also respect the question’s phase.
Two implementation choices affect which condition is being evaluated:
falling back to dense ordering omits reranking, and changing chunking or
embeddings requires a rebuilt index. Interpreting a score also requires
the dataset version, model identities, experimental settings,
and source-specific exclusions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">D.5. Scoring and benchmark-level summaries</h3>

<section id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Benchmark weights.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LoCoMo averages correctness over the 1,540 included questions and
the specified judge repetitions. LongMemEval averages over its 500
examples under the stated judging procedure. MEME reports the macro-average
of the six task scores. PersonaMem-v2 uses question-weighted
multiple-choice accuracy over 5,000 questions.
MemoryAgentBench first computes source scores and then the four
capability scores. In Test-Time Learning, the five ICL source scores
are averaged before being combined with ReDial:</p>
