Full-benchmark results require complete source coverage.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">D.6. Latency measurement</h3>

<div id="A4.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Sequential-ingestion measurements record the duration of each message’s
ingestion. The BEAM comparison includes complete runs
over the same 1,710-message conversation, and cumulative Add time is
the sum of the recorded message-level durations. Both conversation-level
and outer ingestion concurrency are set to one:
each single-message Add finishes before the next starts, with native
internal parallelism unchanged. For MemMachine,
the timer also includes a semantic-ingestion readiness barrier,
polled every second with a one-hour timeout; its sequential-run
configuration triggers semantic ingestion after one message rather
than waiting for a multi-message batch.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The separate MEME latency experiment measures Add-to-ready and Search
over 10 conversations and 110 questions, with both outer conversation
and search concurrency set to one. MemOS uses synchronous Add;
MemMachine readiness is determined by an empty pending-ingestion
queue rather than the ordinary fixed post-Add wait.
Total latency sums the unrounded Add-to-ready and Search times, excluding
overhead outside these stages; the displayed entries are rounded independently.
Neither timing experiment includes reader generation or evaluation.
Both run on the host described in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS7" title="D.7. Computational environment ‣ Appendix D Experimental Implementation and Reproducibility ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.7</span></a>.
Within the measured stages, wall-clock time includes local computation
and storage, API-service latency, network communication, and retries.
The comparison is influenced by each system’s readiness rules and internal
parallelism, as well as the available CPU, memory, disk, container
resources, and endpoint load.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">D.7. Computational environment</h3>

<section id="A4.SS7.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Deployment configuration.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All experiments conducted in this study, including the latency and
sequential-ingestion experiments, were orchestrated on the same local
machine. Evaluation processes, local memory stores, and the self-hosted
MemOS and MemMachine services ran on that machine. LLM, embedding, and
reranking inference used API endpoints. Published comparison scores were
obtained in the environments described by their respective studies.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS7.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Hardware.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The host has an Intel Core i9-13900HX CPU with 24 physical cores and
32 logical processors, 16 GiB of installed RAM, an NVIDIA GeForce
RTX 5070 Ti Laptop GPU with approximately 12 GB of dedicated memory,
and a 1 TB YMTC PC411 NVMe SSD. The recorded host software is 64-bit
Windows 11 Home, build 26200, with NVIDIA driver 581.29.
The GPU accelerates enabled local operations, notably
MemU’s exact item-similarity search.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS7.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Software environment.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS7.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS7.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Methods with incompatible dependencies run in separate environments.
The general evaluation environment uses Python 3.12 and PyTorch 2.9.1
with CUDA 12.8. MemU uses Python 3.13, MemU 1.5.1, PostgreSQL with
pgvector, and PyTorch-based exact similarity search rather than
FAISS-GPU. SimpleMem uses Python 3.12, OpenAI client 2.3.0,
LanceDB 0.25.3, PyArrow 22.0.0, and pylance 0.39.0; its embeddings
are obtained through model APIs. Memora uses Chroma for persistent
storage and BM25 for lexical retrieval.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS7.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS7.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Mem0 mutation audit and ADD/NONE intervention use Mem0 1.0.11,
whereas the MEME store used for evidence analysis uses Mem0 2.0.11. MemOS and MemMachine
run as locally hosted Docker services. Software versions and operating
conditions are given where recorded; some historical dependency versions
are unavailable.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Review of Prior RAG Evaluations</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix reviews raw and naive RAG baselines in prior studies.
Weak baseline configurations may partly explain the reported poor performance of RAG. These observations motivate stronger retrieval controls.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection">E.1. Review scope</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We examine retrieval-design limitations that may explain weak historical RAG
baselines relative to <span id="A5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span>: overly large chunks, overly low top-<math id="A5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and
absent reranking. We examine studies that independently evaluated raw or
naive RAG, along with the public RAG configuration accompanying Memobase’s
LoCoMo evaluation. For Memobase, we examine only the configuration.
The published reference scores use different readers, prompts, question
sets, budgets, and scoring across papers.</p>
</div>
</section>
