<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Extended Discussion and Limitations</h2>

<section id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Operational memory management.</h5>

<div id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The evaluated tasks ask for answers from recorded histories. Operational
memory systems may also need to expose authoritative state to other tools, trigger
actions after updates, or erase stored information. These requirements
introduce memory-management objectives beyond answer accuracy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reader dependence.</h5>

<div id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The large, task-dependent reader differences in the phase-visible MEME
oracle (Table <a href="#S1.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) show that state reconstruction depends
on reader capability. Cross-system utilization estimates also depend on
which questions each system supplies with sufficient evidence.
Whether state construction during ingestion helps weaker readers or reduces
repeated inference remains an open question. Retrieval should be evaluated
with the intended reader.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Demonstration structure.</h5>

<div id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">MemoryAgentBench’s test-time-learning split combines few-shot
classification with recommendation. For classification, usable
demonstrations require factual content, recognizable
input–label pairings, example boundaries, and presentation format.
Atomic-fact extraction or summarization can disrupt that format even
when much of the underlying content survives. The drop from 47.68%
to 15.37% under atomic-fact extraction may reflect reduced evidence
volume at fixed item count, disrupted demonstration structure, and
reader–format mismatch, alongside factual loss. Both token volume and
demonstration format vary in this ablation; the separate six-system 5k
control in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4. Ablation studies ‣ Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a> leaves these factors
coupled. The result shows that the tested representation and reader
combination performs poorly on TTL.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Scalability and generalizability.</h5>

<div id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Raw storage grows with history length, and a fixed candidate and
reader budget can miss dispersed or low-similarity evidence. Turn
expansion and overlapping windows may also spend context on repeated
text. Retrieval quality across history lengths and the cost of repeated
query-time reasoning remain to be measured alongside ingestion.
Hosted model and service behavior can change, and outputs can vary
across temperature-zero requests and repeated judgments.
Longer interactive workloads, bounded-storage settings, and broader
multimodal evidence may favor different memory-management trade-offs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
