<section id="A7.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Failure attribution.</h5>

<div id="A7.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The historical evaluation records contain binary scores. Raw judge
responses and per-judgment parse and request status are unavailable.
A zero may represent a valid WRONG judgment, a formatting
failure, or a failed request, leaving the exact format-failure count
unknown. For the four <span id="A7.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Raw-RAG</span> conditions in
Tables <a href="#A1.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ A.1.1. Text-embedding-3-small setting ‣ A.1. LoCoMo results ‣ Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>
and <a href="#A1.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ A.1.2. Qwen3 retrieval setting ‣ A.1. LoCoMo results ‣ Appendix A Experimental Settings and Complete Results ‣ Is Complex Agentic Memory Always Needed? Naive RAG Is Stronger Than We Thought" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, we verify all 4,620 expected binary
judgments per condition (18,480 in total), with zero missing judgment
slots. Their zero-score counts are 505 and 291 for the
text-embedding-3-small conditions, and 502 and 252 for the
Qwen3 conditions, in GPT-4o-mini/GPT-4.1-mini reader order.
For each condition, the number of format failures is bounded between
zero and its zero-score count. Scores are reported unchanged; recovering
the historical failure rate would require the original responses and
diagnostic status.</p>
</div>
</section>
