<section id="A7.SS10.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Coverage labels.</h5>

<div id="A7.SS10.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS10.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the Tr-after and Cas-after comparison, value_complete is
result coverage and sufficient is path coverage. The analysis checks that
no path-positive row is result-negative before computing
<math id="A7.SS10.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\mathrm{path}\mid\mathrm{result})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>path</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>result</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\mathrm{path}\mid\mathrm{result})</annotation></semantics></math>. It uses the judge’s sufficiency
label directly. Tr requires the complete history plus ordering; Cas requires the
new target plus a linked dependency and trigger. Del and Abs are excluded
from this nested result/path comparison because a deleted or invalidated
old value is invalid as a current result. They remain part of the broader
task-aware assessment. Both labels in the nested comparison come from the
joint full-context judgment.</p>
</div>
